cao atencao clawson varadi cavalcante vasconcelos ceccim maciel henriques rlm inm mrf leagues botucatu especialistas reconhecimento sinais bvsalud biblioref 90306a0237c000d33f4dbd3f8f41ba7c homologa portaria atualiza especialidades atuacao aprovadas comissao mista especialidades diario uniao p145 junho godinho juri simulado estrategica metodologica ensino remoto alteracoes cromossomicas relato experiencia ideias v13i3 vitiello horovitz conhecimento atitudes s0100 cufer kosty e2300277 hayflick fantin alencar ndm benzaquem prazeres vmg conhecimento cidade manaus amazonas sustinere sustinere forsyth servint shilkofski bodurtha goergen ligas academicas revisao varias experiencias catarinenses arquivos gramasco hhf baptista avo lrs germano biologia mestrado profissionalizante aconselhamento genetico mpag iriart jab nucci muniz viana aureliano gibbon busca incertezas desafios geneticas cienc coletiva karam conhecimento docentes academicos periodicos unemat revistamedicina 33s 38s veinot mylopoulos overshadowed cmej taar teambasedlearning tblc20 lipscomb galvez peralta cropp crutchley calinski iwuchukwu ajpe llerena mcn concepcao familia religiosidade discursos produzidos profissionais acerca criancas geneticas cienc coletiva s1413 avo germano demarzo s12687 husny ferraz vef 5271v43suplemento1 mennin rhp lacaz ligas academicas formacao exploratorio numa tradicional escola 52712015v43n1rb20170141 karam yamamura avo germano enfermagem percepcoes profissionais envolvidos processo ensino aprendizagem e121 5271v46 eed4362 s1679 45082018ed4362 porciuncula cgg ensino cursos estadual campinas faculdade unicamp riegert alford broder keats bjb pyeritz 52712015v43n3rb20180256 kishi rgb dlg narciso trf avo germano sandes identificacao geneticas atencao experiencia municipio porte comunidade rbmfc15 sbegg enfermagem genomica sbegg sbgm genomica profissionais atuam atencao sbgm cartilha sbgm genomica sbgm sboc oncologia cancerologia baseado competencias oncologia com_docman alias cancerologia sesu category_slug agosto itemid scheffer guilloux demografia fmusp demografiamedica2023_8fev talwar shagrawi telessauders ufrgs curso ead geneticas ufrgs telessauders cursos keels calzone badzek dewell tonkin genes14112013 leagues comunicacao s1414 dkr pessoas deficiencia atencao editora fiocruz dkr horovitz llerena itinerante criancas adolescentes deficiencia vinculadas familia familia comunidade rbmfc rbmfc dkr attianezi horovitz llerena atencao experiencia municipio porte physis s0103','Abstract This manuscript reviewed the state of the art about the teaching and training of human resources for genetics and genomics in Brazil. We presented the national scenario of teaching genetics in medical undergraduate and other health courses. We discussed the training of medical geneticists through medical residency and addressed the training in genetics of physicians from specialties other than genetics. We examined the training of health professionals specializing in genetics through lato sensu and stricto sensu postgraduate programs and presented the proposals for multi-professional residency in genetic counseling and genetics and genomics that are currently the subject of discussion in the country. Finally, we highlighted the importance of training primary health care professionals concerning genetics and genomics for the effective establishment of a line of care for individuals with genetic disorders in the Brazilian Unified Health System. Therefore, we provided a thorough overview of how genetics is (or is not) incorporated into professional training in a comprehensive public healthcare system such as the Brazilian. Keywords: Health workforce, Medical education, Interprofessional education, Continuing education, Medical genetics, Brazil Introduction It is estimated that congenital anomalies occur in 2–5% of live births, representing the second leading cause of infant mortality in Brazil (Brasil 2021a ). Furthermore, with the advancement of diagnostic and treatment technologies for genetic diseases, the demand for genetic knowledge in healthcare has increased gradually. In 2014, the Ministry of Health established the National Policy for Comprehensive Care for People with Rare Diseases in the Unified Health System (in Portuguese: Sistema Único de Saúde, SUS) (Brasil 2014a ), aiming to enhance patients’ access to medical genetics clinics. Within this framework, healthcare professionals must receive adequate training to effectively integrate genetics and genomics into healthcare delivery. In this manuscript, we reviewed the state of the art about the teaching and training of human resources for genetics and genomics in Brazil. Teaching genetics in medical undergraduate Teaching genetics in medical undergraduate courses in Brazil began in 1959 with a human genetics course at the Institute of Biosciences of the University of São Paulo. Over the years, the incorporation of genetic topics into medical undergraduate courses has occurred non-systematically (Beiguelman 2000 ; Porciúncula 2004 ). One of the earliest studies on the subject was conducted by Porciúncula ( 2004 ), covering 105 medical undergraduate schools in the country at the time; data was available from approximately half of them. In most schools, there was an academic discipline of genetics, usually associated with the basic sciences (Porciúncula 2004 ). Currently, there are 379 medical schools in Brazil, the majority established after 2000 (Brasil, Ministério da Educação 2024a ). We examined the pedagogical projects and curriculum available on the websites of each school to outline the availability of genetics and/or molecular biology and medical genetics disciplines. The survey revealed that genetic content is still mainly presented during the preclinical years and encompasses only basic genetics; few institutions offer a medical genetics discipline after the second year, and only one institution offers an internship in medical genetics (Table 1 ). Genetic themes are scarcely presented in other disciplines, such as pediatrics. Interestingly, even though the increment in the accreditation of medical schools coincides with the expansion of knowledge and uptake of genetic and genomic techniques in healthcare, how this information is provided to students has not changed, i.e., it is still based on the basic cycle (Porciúncula 2004 ). Undoubtedly, the complexity of the medical curriculum has increased in recent decades, and courses need to cover various important topics within a limited number of hours over six years. In this scenario, integrating new disciplines into the medical curriculum is a challenging task. Table 1. Distribution of genetics content in medical undergraduate courses according to pedagogical projects and curricular matrices available on the websites of different institutions ( N = 379) Educational institution according to administrative category Preclinical years n (%) Clinical years n (%) Optional discipline n (%) Discipline of genetics Genetics within another discipline Discipline of Cellular and Molecular Biology No information a Discipline of genetics Genetics within another discipline Genetics in internship Genetics in internship within other disciplines No information a Medical genetics Human genetics No information a Public ( n = 142) 35 (24.6%) 69 (48.6%) 14 (9.8%) 19 (13.4%) 17 (11.9%) 34 (23.9%) 0 4 (2.8%) 19 (13.4%) 9 (6.3%) 7 (4.9%) 19 (13.4%) Private ( n = 237) 52 (21.9%) 51 (21.5%) 50 (21.1%) 125 (52.7%) 8 (3.4%) 6 (2.5%) 1 (0.4%) 0 125 (52.7%) 5 (2.1%) 5 (2.1%) 130 (54.9%) Total ( n = 379) 87 (22.9%) 120 (31.7%) 64 (16.9%) 144 (38%) 25 (6.6%) 40 (10.5%) 1 (0.3%) 4 (1%) 144 (38%) 14 (3.7%) 12 (3.2%) 149 (39.3%) Open in a new tab a It was not possible to retrieve the information because the course pedagogical projects and curricular grids were not available, or because, even with the curricular grid available, it was not possible to identify the content of medical genetics or molecular biology in the curriculum. Some of the 379 medical courses offer more than one discipline related to genetics, medical genetics, molecular biology, or genetics topics incorporated into other disciplines In recent years, the training process for human resources in healthcare has undergone considerable changes, aiming to link theory and practice through active methodologies to develop critical-reflexive professionals (Belei et al. 2008 ). Although there is a recent trend towards integrating content from the preclinical and clinical years, which impairs the incorporation of clinical competencies related to genetics into healthcare (Korf 2002 ), the teaching of genetics in the clinical years is still limited in Brazil. The latest national curriculum guidelines for medicine, defined by the Ministry of Education in 2014, state that the general physician should be able to refer cases to genetic counseling (Brasil 2014b ). To achieve this educational goal, in 2019, the Brazilian Society of Medical Genetics and Genomics (in Portuguese: Sociedade Brasileira de Genética e Genômica, SBGM) established a minimum competency profile in genetics, listing essential knowledge, skills, and attitudes for Brazilian physicians (Melo et al. 2019 ). The challenge is to expand the scenarios for students to engage with genetics throughout their undergraduate studies, rather than just offer an isolated discipline in the preclinical years. In this regard, there are some interesting extracurricular initiatives considered complementary activities by the national curriculum guidelines for medicine, such as academic leagues, community-aimed projects, scientific initiation, tutoring, and optional clinical internships (Brasil 2014b ). These activities range from experiences organized by faculties with mandatory requirements to experiences organized by students themselves. They are considered opportunities to diversify teaching-learning environments, explore possible future professional choices, and address perceived shortcomings in the official curriculum (Li et al. 2023 ). Since these can be informal activities, it is difficult to assess their uptake in Brazil. Among these initiatives, academic leagues (or learning communities) stand out as non-profit student organizations that aim to provide opportunities for non-mandatory theoretical and practical activities in a specific area of knowledge under faculty guidance (Torres 2008 ; Goergen et al. 2017 ; Moreira et al. 2019 ; Cavalcante et al. 2021 ). Currently, in Brazil, there are approximately 30 academic leagues related to genetics in the Brazilian Association of Academic Leagues of Medical Genetics and Rare Diseases (Letícia Santiago 2024, personal communication). Despite no longer questioning the value of genetics and genomics in medicine, many students maintain a misrepresented view of the specialty, believing that genetic diseases are rare and untreatable and that only a few specialists will deal with them in their clinical practice, such as pediatricians. It is important to encourage professors to transform their lectures into provocative and participative sessions, focusing on andragogy and student-centered approaches, which improve content retention and promote more meaningful and efficient learning (Pereira and Lima 2018 ). Successful national experiences report the use of active teaching-learning strategies such as simulated juries, memory games, problem-based learning, and team-based learning in genetics education (Santana et al. 2019 ; Lima et al. 2020 ; Chaves et al. 2022 ; Andrade and Nobrega 2023 ). Basic genetics content must be linked to clinical practice. We believe that the presence of genetics outpatient clinics in universities would facilitate the introduction of complex topics such as rare diseases and genomic diagnostic techniques for students, allowing not only future physicians to become acquainted with the specialty and recognize its utility, but also facilitating the spread of the specialty throughout the country. Teaching genetics in different undergraduate healthcare courses Genetics and genomics are also relevant for non-medical healthcare courses. Nonetheless, the uptake of genetic themes in the curriculum of these health professionals is even more challenging. The national curricular guidelines for undergraduate courses in health sciences other than medicine touch on genetics teaching in different ways. The national curriculum guidelines for biomedicine state that principles of human genetics should be contemplated, and the graduate must be able to perform, interpret, and write reports and be held technically responsible for molecular biology laboratory analysis (Brasil 2003 ). For pharmacy, national curriculum guidelines regard genetic analysis and molecular biology as professional practice needs and consider that the pharmacist should be trained in research and development for innovation, production, evaluation, regulation, and quality control of all products produced by pharmacogenetics and pharmacogenomics (Brasil 2017 ). For nursing, nutrition, physical therapy, speech-language therapy, occupational therapy, and dentistry, national curriculum guidelines mention that curriculum should include the molecular basis of health and disease (Brasil 2001a , b , 2002a , b , c , 2021b ). For physical education undergraduates, human genetics applied to physical education is necessary for graduation (Brasil 2018 ). However, for psychology (Brasil 2023 ) and collective health (Brasil 2022 ), there is no information regarding human genetics, genomics, or the molecular basis of diseases. Despite low awareness of genetics in the nursing curriculum, the Brazilian Society of Nursing in Genetics and Genomics (in Portuguese: Sociedade Brasileira de Enfermagem em Genética e Genômica, SBEGG) was established in 2017 (SBEGG 2024 ). At that moment, 20 nurses, most with postgraduate degrees in genetics, were members of the society. Nowadays, about 50 professionals are members of SBEGG (Erika Santos 2023, personal communication). A British longitudinal study enrolled 153 nurses between 2019 and 2022 and revealed that even though 80% recognize the importance of genomics in patient care, only 40% were confident in their knowledge of the subject, which reveals the knowledge gap in this professional group (Carpenter-Clawson et al. 2023 ). A Brazilian study that enrolled health care professors – 70% physicians, 17.5% nurses, and 12.5% from other health professions – disclosed that 77.5% trusted that their activities were indirectly related to genetics, and most of them were able to identify clinically relevant data that should lead to medical genetics evaluation; however, they felt insecure about requesting, interpreting, and communicating genetic testing results (Moreira et al. 2022 ). Studies about teaching and comprehension of genetics and genomics in different health professions courses are scarce in Brazil and abroad; most publications concern medicine and nursing (Thomas et al. 2023 ), and more recently, pharmacy, probably because of advances in pharmacogenomics (Lipscomb et al. 2023 ). The role of genomics in health care is increasingly recognized. Therefore, other health professionals need to incorporate this discipline into their curriculum so they may evaluate the effectiveness of learning among their professionals and its effect on patient care (Talwar et al. 2019 ). Training specialist doctors in medical genetics: medical residency Postgraduate training in the form of medical residency is offered to physicians and is characterized by practical training, tutored by highly qualified and ethical physicians, under the responsibility of health institutions that may be linked to universities or not (Brasil, Ministério da Saúde 2024b ). Regulation and supervision of medical residency programs fall under the National Medical Residency Commission (in Portuguese: Comissão Nacional de Residência Médica, CNRM), which congregates representatives from the Ministry of Health, the Ministry of Education, the Medical Federal Board, and the Brazilian Medical Association. The Ministry of Education and the Ministry of Health are responsible for funding the program across the country through scholarships for medical residents (Brasil, Ministério da Educação 2024c ). Nowadays, the Federal Medical Board recognizes 55 medical specialties, including medical genetics, and 62 sub-specialties (CFM 2024 ). The first medical genetics residency program in Brazil was created in 1977 at Ribeirão Preto Clinics Hospital, from the University of São Paulo. This happened before the recognition of medical genetics as a specialty by the Brazilian Medical Association and the Federal Medical Board and the officialization of medical residency by the Ministry of Health. The lack of recognition of medical genetics as a specialty put the course at risk and prompted the acknowledgment of the specialty in 1983 and the foundation of the Brazilian Society of Medical Genetics and Genomics in 1986 (SBGM 2024 ). Nowadays, 12 medical genetics residency programs offer 28 training places each year in the country. Physicians may access the program straight from medical graduation, and the course lasts three years. After medical residency, some hospitals offer further training ( lato sensu ) in specific areas of medical genetics, such as inborn errors of metabolism, oncogenetics, or neurogenetics, which lasts one or two years. Figure 1 summarizes the distribution of those programs in the country and shows that most of them are in the south and southeast regions, while Table 2 lists the Institutions that offer residency programs in medical genetics, including the respective cities, institutions, and the number of available positions. All Institutions are affiliated with public hospitals; 11 of them are university institutions. Their presence in the public health system is regarded as an advantage because of the great number and diversity of cases, the interconnection with the national neonatal screening program, and the possibility of offering complex medical care to the population. In contrast, due to fragilities in the SUS, access to modern genomic techniques in the public health system is limited, especially in those institutions that have not been accredited by the National Policy for Comprehensive Care for People with Rare Diseases. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Distribution of the 12 residency programs in Medical Genetics in Brazil. The numbers inside the circles represent the number of positions available per year in each state of the country Table 2. List of institutions that offer residency programs in medical genetics, including the respective cities and the number of available positions City/State Institution Number of positions offered per year Fortaleza/CE Hospital Universitário do Ceará / Escola de Saúde Pública do Ceará (HU/ESP CE) 02 Salvador/BA Complexo Hospital Universitário Professor Edgar Santos / Maternidade Climério de Oliveira / Universidade Federal da Bahia (HUPES/MCO/UFBA) 03 Brasília/DF Escola Superior de Ciências da Saúde (ESCS) 02 Belo Horizonte/MG Hospital das Clínicas da Universidade Federal de Minhas Gerais (UFMG) 02 Rio de Janeiro/RJ Hospital Universitário Gaffrée e Guinle / Universidade Federal do Estado do Rio de Janeiro (HUGG/UNIRIO) 02 Rio de Janeiro/RJ Instituto Fernandes Figueira / Fundação Oswaldo Cruz (IFF/FIOCRUZ) 02 São Paulo/SP Hospital das Clínicas da Faculdade de Medicina da Universidade de São Paulo (HCFMUSP) 04 São Paulo/SP Universidade Federal de São Paulo / Escola Paulista de Medicina (UNIFESP/EPM) 02 Campinas/SP Universidade Estadual de Campinas (UNICAMP) 02 Ribeirão Preto/SP Hospital das Clínicas da Faculdade de Medicina de Ribeirão Preto da Universidade de São Paulo (HCFMRP-USP) 03 Porto Alegre/RS Hospital de Clínicas de Porto Alegre (HCPA) 03 Porto Alegre/RS Universidade Federal de Ciências da Saúde de Porto Alegre / Irmandade da Santa Casa de Misericórdia de Porto Alegre (UFCSPA) 01 Open in a new tab The current competency profile for medical residency in medical genetics was approved in April 2019, after construction by representatives from all active residency programs at that time, coordinated by SBGM. The group defined requirements for the formation of a medical geneticist, able to deal with all areas of the specialty, bearing in mind theory, practice, and ethical aspects. Proper training in genetic counseling is one of the skills expected to be developed during the residency program (Brasil, Ministério da Educação 2019 ). A survey with residency supervisors in the last four years revealed that most of the curriculum is being fulfilled, even though some aspects are still deficient. Major challenges are encountered in the fields of oncogenetics, inborn errors of metabolism, neonatal screening, reproductive medicine, and molecular biology. Resources to overcome those obstacles include internships in different residency programs, outpatient clinics, private and public laboratories, and remote tutoring in molecular biology (Correia 2023, personal communication). Medical genetics is the specialty with the lowest number of doctors in Brazil; only 342 are officially recognized by the Federal Medical Board (Scheffer et al. 2023 ). With the increasing demand for medical geneticists, it is vital that the number of scholarships and medical residency programs also expand, particularly in the north, northeast, and central-west regions. Cooperation between residency programs, clinics, and public and private labs is of utmost importance, so the qualifications of medical residents may be outstanding. Training doctors from other specialties in medical genetics International curriculum guidelines recommend that genetic counseling is mandatory in the training of other medical specialties, for example in the internal medicine residency program in the United States of America (USA) (Riegert-Johnson et al. 2004 ) and in the joint proposal from the American Society of Clinical Oncology and the European Society for Medical Oncology for oncology residency (Cufer et al. 2023 ); or optional, as recommended for pediatrics residency in the USA (Forsyth et al. 2020 ). Education on genetics and genomics among non-geneticist physicians is still scarce in Brazil. Since 2017, the National Medical Residency Commission, in partnership with medical specialty societies in the country, has introduced a competency-based model for all medical residencies in Brazil. After excluding the medical genetics residency program, an examination of those documents revealed that 17 medical specialties and 14 subspecialties (11 pediatric-related) consider genetics and genomics training relevant for medical residents. Among those, knowledge of genetic testing is the most frequent expertise desired, being relevant to 12 specialties and 5 subspecialties. Recognition and diagnosis of genetic diseases were mentioned by 7 specialties and 6 subspecialties. Furthermore, management of genetic diseases was indicated by 4 specialties and 8 subspecialties, and basic genetic knowledge was mentioned by 7 specialties and 7 subspecialties (Brasil, Ministério da Educação 2023 ). As an example, the Brazilian Society of Clinical Oncology addresses several genetic topics and includes the recognition of cancer predisposition syndromes as an essential skill in the competency profile for clinical oncology residents (SBOC n.d. ). For some residency programs, such as oncology, an internship in medical genetics is mandatory during residency training. These data reveal that few specialties consider teaching genetics to physicians, and those who do so emphasize complex technologies rather than clinical recognition and management of genetic diseases. The Brazilian Society of Medical Genetics and Genomics recommends the awareness and management of genetic diseases and the promotion of preventive measures for genetic diseases and congenital anomalies as fundamental competencies for all physicians in Brazil (Melo et al. 2019 ), which has not been incorporated by other specialties. Data from different studies with doctors in Brazil show that they know about basic genetics. However, expertise in clinical genetics and ethical aspects is still lacking (Correia et al. 2011 ; Fantin et al. 2022 ; Karam and Wada 2020 ). Its effects are represented as delayed access of patients to proper diagnosis and treatments (Iriart et al. 2019 ) and an impact on physicians’ attitudes, which sometimes adopt a paternalistic view (Correia et al. 2011 ) and use religion and moral values to explain genetic disease and disabilities to patients instead of strengthening the individual’s autonomy (Martins et al. 2012 ). Training other health professionals in genetics Postgraduate lato sensu courses The development of genetics education in Brazil is also possible through lato sensu postgraduate courses, which, according to guidelines from the Ministry of Education, should have at least 360 h and may be offered by accredited institutions with experience and installed capacity (Brasil, Ministério da Educação 2007 ). There are many courses in genetics, molecular biology, and cytogenetics, focusing on laboratory training and directed to a diverse public, and some courses about clinical genetics, targeting non-specialist doctors and nurses. The curriculum and appropriateness of these courses vary fairly. Professional master’s degree in genetic counseling and other strict sensu postgraduate programs in human genetics The Coordination for the Improvement of Higher Education Personnel (in Portuguese: Coordenação de Aperfeiçoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior, CAPES) currently registers 27 academic courses of master’s degree and/or doctorate in genetics (Brasil 2024 ), many of which focus on human and medical genetics. In 2015, the University of São Paulo set up a professional master’s degree in Genetic Counseling and Human Genomics. This is a pioneering initiative in the country, aimed at non-medical professionals who graduated in biology, biomedicine, nursing, psychology, social services, physical therapy, and other health-related areas. The purpose of this is to train professionals to work in the field of health care in the services of genetic counseling, cytogenetics laboratories, and human molecular genetics (Instituto de Biologia, Universidade de São Paulo n.d. ). It is difficult to oversee the curriculum matrix of those courses, as each postgraduate program and each institution have the liberty to define disciplines, objectives, and learning strategies. Besides, there is not a close dialogue and institutional mechanisms established to articulate postgraduate programs accredited by the Ministry of Education alongside the Ministry of Health and the needs of SUS. For this reason, the Ministry of Health has invested in multiprofessional residencies as a way to train health specialists. Multiprofessional residency in genetic counseling and genetics and genomics Multiprofessional residency was enacted by law n°11.129 on June 30th 2005, and it is a lato sensu postgraduate course designed for non-medical health professions (Brasil, Ministério da Saúde 2024d ). Those actions are aimed at education in practice, guided by the principles and guidelines of SUS, and originate from needs and regional realities, including different health professions: biomedicine, biology, physical education, nursing, pharmacy, physical therapy, speech-language pathology, veterinary medicine, nutrition and dietetics, dentistry, psychology, social services, occupational therapy, collective health, and medical physics. As in the case of medical residencies, the organization and functioning of multiprofessional residencies are regulated by a joint act between the Ministry of Education and the Ministry of Health, with scholarships offered by the Ministry of Education (Brasil, Ministério da Educação 2024d ). In 2020, stimulated by the National Policy for Comprehensive Care for People with Rare Diseases in SUS (Brasil 2014a ), the Ministry of Health set up a working group to develop two multiprofessional residency projects related to genetics: one in genetic counseling and another in genetics and genomics. In Brazil, the non-medical genetic counselor is not a regulated profession, even though the National Policy for Comprehensive Care for People with Rare Diseases in SUS supports genetic counseling as a multidisciplinary activity (Brasil 2014a ). In the private sector, genetic counseling activity is included in the Hierarchized Brazilian Classification of Medical Procedures (in Portuguese: Classificação Brasileira Hierarquizada de Procedimentos Médicos, CBHPM), a chart that specifies payment parameters for each health-related activity and is classified as a medical activity (AMB 2012 ). The national program of multidisciplinary residency in genetic counseling targets psychologists, nurses, biologists, and biomedicals. It lasts two years and demands a commitment of 60 h per week. The pedagogical matrix is composed of up to 20% theoretical-practical activities, and the remainder of the time is dedicated to practical activities. This matrix was planned to be implemented in specialized clinics where genetic counseling already takes place, such as Medical Genetics services licensed by the National Policy for Comprehensive Care for People with Rare Diseases in SUS and services where medical genetics residency programs are set up, to stimulate multidisciplinary teamwork. Given the background diversity among the many professionals who may access this residency, the curriculum has a wide scope, from the organization of the human genome and variant analysis to the communication of bad news and the fundamentals of ethics and psychology. A multidisciplinary residency program in genetics and genomics targets biomedicals, biologists, nurses, pharmacists, physical therapists, dietitians, speech-language pathologists, dentists, psychologists, and social workers. It also lasts two years and demands a commitment of 60 h per week, which are distributed up to 20% to theoretical-practical-scientific activities and the remainder to practical activities, summing up to 1,152 h dedicated to theory and 4,608 h to practice. It aims to qualify different health professionals to operate in services that offer assistance to patients with genetic conditions to encourage multiprofessional teamwork within their scope of action. Even though both projects have been designed, due to macro-political aspects, including coping with the Covid-19 pandemic, they have not yet been authorized by the National Commission of Multiprofessional Residency in Health. The discussion about those residencies within the Ministry of Education and Ministry of Health is soon expected. Training primary care health professionals in genetics Tasks of primary health care professionals (PHCP) within the field of genetics include identifying patients with genetic diseases based on the clinical history and physical examination; communicating on cancer predisposition; referring patients to medical genetics clinics; conducting patients with genetic disease alongside the medical geneticist; guiding patients and families on basic genetic information; supporting their decisions; coordinating the care of a person with complex requirements; and recognizing the psychosocial demands of families of patients with genetic diseases (Emery and Hayflick 2001 ). In Brazil, primary care is the main entry point to SUS. It is organized in a decentralized manner, with clinics located near people’s homes. There are multiple government strategies related to primary healthcare; one of those is the family healthcare strategy, which takes multidisciplinary care to communities through Family Health Units (Brasil, Ministério da Saúde 2024e ). The National Policy for Comprehensive Care for People with Rare Diseases in SUS anticipates specific assignments to primary healthcare in conformity with global guidelines on this subject (Brasil 2014a ). There are some experiences with the identification of people with genetic diseases in primary care in Brazil. In general, those studies recognize primary care as an important entry point to SUS, which reduces public costs with out-of-municipality treatment without damaging access to specialized clinics. At the same time, they observe the need to expand permanent education actions in genetics to healthcare professionals in primary care (Vieira et al. 2012 , 2013 ; Acosta et al. 2013 ; Vieira 2019 ; Santos et al. 2020 ). The rarity of diseases adds to the unfamiliarity of health professionals with those diseases. They are not able to suspect genetic disease and therefore do not refer to specialized care (Melo et al. 2015 ; Gramasco et al. 2016 ; Iriart et al. 2019 ). Referral seems even harder in rural areas, as, many times, health professionals ignore the existence of genetic clinics in SUS (Iriart et al. 2019 ). Besides, the continuity of care is impaired when healthcare professionals in primary care are not well qualified to deal with complex demands (Vieira et al. 2012 , 2013 ; Acosta et al. 2013 ; Vieira 2019 ; Santos et al. 2020 ). SBGM, in cooperation with the Federal Medical Board and public universities, has been investing in training programs in genetics for primary care professionals, which includes telehealth strategies (CFM 2018 ; TelessaúdeRS 2020 ; SBGM 2023 ). Increasing genetics clinics in SUS is a great challenge that must be overcome by the effective implementation of the National Policy for Comprehensive Care for People with Rare Diseases. Conclusions With this review, we provided a thorough overview of the topic that can help understand how genetics is (or is not) incorporated in a complex public healthcare system such as the Brazilian one. Major challenges in the national setting include the promotion of genetics education in the context of clinical care in undergraduate courses, therefore ensuring proper background on genetics for all health care professionals; expanding medical genetics residency programs to increase the number of specialists in Brazil; regulating the profession of genetic counselor and multiprofessional residency in genetic counseling and genetics and genomics; and qualifying primary care professionals in genetics. The effective implementation of the National Policy for Comprehensive Care for People with Rare Diseases depends on overcoming these obstacles. Author contributions D.G.M. designed the review, with support from F.T.L., M.A.F.D.L., P.S.C., R.S.H., R.E.M., and S.O.K. All authors wrote the first draft of the manuscript. All authors criticized and revised the previous versions of the manuscript. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Funding The authors received no financial support for the research, authorship, and/or publication of this article. Data availability No datasets were generated or analysed during the current study. Code availability Code sharing is not applicable to this article as no code was generated during the current study. Declarations Ethics approval and consent to participate Not applicable. This article does not contain any studies with human or animal subjects performed by any of the authors. Competing interests The authors declare no competing interests. Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References Acosta AX, Abé-Sandes K, Giugliani R, Bittles AH (2013) Delivering genetic education and genetic counseling for rare diseases in rural Brazil. J Genet Couns 22:830–834. 10.1007/s10897-013-9570-x [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] AMB – Associação Médica Brasileira (2012) Classificação Brasileira Hierarquizada de Procedimentos Médicos. https://sbacvsp.com.br/Procedimentos/Tabela-CBHPM-Geral.pdf . Accessed 09 February 2024 Andrade RLS, Nobrega MAS (2023) Me