INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(6746,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8368169','Simulation: an Innovative Approach to Engaging Preclinical Medical Students with Bioethics.','Bishop CE, Maradiaga G, Freeman KR, Peters TR, Jackson JM.','2021','rehearse contextualizing presession presession forthe forthe glean maradiaga irb00050949 filebase 20_functions structure_2018 kasoff federman giubilini milnes carrese romanell eckles meslin helft goldie persad sedig lakhan hamlat mcnamee humanit geppert hazuda stites clapp fiester empir wcis 0b013e318217e119 jjcs furlong pits cdep kreiter s15328015tlm1403_2 boggs sdt fadel ferretti announcing gfe rohde muirhead daboval daboval tfe rohde muirhead ferretti rohde muirhead daboval lupi cbm runyan ccmj woll chipman lupi csn runyan kch sourkes robb gisondi coggins soltysik yarnold mylopoulos kulasegaram kulasegaram howey neville dore kulasegaram dore neville','Abstract Background Integrating bioethical concepts into preclinical medical school curriculum and engaging early medical learners in bioethics are a challenge. Activity A total of 140 medical students participated in a 2-h simulation activity consisting of a series of standardized patient (SP) encounters. Results A total of 41 of 140 students (29%) completed the learner evaluation survey. Ninety-one percent thought that the SP encounter was relevant to their role as a future physician. Ninety-three percent of students rated the exercise as highly effective. Conclusions SP encounters enhance preclinical medical students’ engagement with bioethics and provide learners practice applying these concepts to clinically relevant scenarios. Keywords: Simulation, Standardized patient, Medical ethics, Undergraduate medical education, Bioethics, Informed consent, Decision-making capacity Background Understanding bioethical concepts is essential for physicians to provide effective, ethically sound, patient-centered care. The Liaison Committee on Medical Education (LCME) mandates that medical school curricula include “instruction for medical students in medical ethics and human values both prior to and during their participation in patient care activities and require medical students to behave ethically in caring for patients and in relating to patients’ families and others involved in patient care” [ 1 ]. The current approach to ethics instruction among US medical schools varies. Common instructional methods include lectures, case-based discussions in large- or small-group settings, and written reflections [ 2 – 4 ], but there is no consensus about best practices for ethic instruction of medical student learners [ 4 – 8 ]. Integrating bioethics content into medical school curricula remains a challenge for educators due to concerns about limited space in the curriculum and limited support for bioethics instruction [ 2 , 9 ]. Limited learner engagement has also been a challenge due to students’ perception that bioethics instruction is less important than other coursework and clinical experiences [ 2 , 9 ]. Additionally, preclinical students often struggle to translate abstract bioethics concepts to practical applications addressing clinical situations [ 10 , 11 ]. Some training programs have piloted simulation-based methods including role-play exercises or standardized patient (SP) encounters involving ethics concepts. Simulation-based instruction applies adult learning principles by providing learners with a clinical context to apply conceptual knowledge; simulation also offers learners opportunities to rehearse and receive feedback on their performance through deliberate practice before applying these skills in real patient encounters [ 12 ]. Importantly, simulation results in high levels of learner engagement, which is a strong predictor of learning outcomes [ 13 , 14 ]. Nearly all studies describing simulation for bioethics training have targeted learner groups in the clinical years of medical school, residency, or fellowship [ 15 – 23 ] rather than preclinical medical students, or have focused on assessment rather than bioethics instruction [ 21 , 24 , 25 ]. In most preclinical curricula, SP encounters are used primarily for teaching and assessing clinical skills rather than for bioethics integration. Learner evaluations of our institution’s bioethics curriculum indicated a need for additional real-world, patient encounter applications of abstract concepts discussed in the course. To provide our preclinical learners the opportunity to practice applying these concepts during realistic patient encounters, we developed and piloted a simulation-based instructional event through which students performed decision-making capacity assessment and informed consent. The objective of this study was to assess learners’ evaluation of the relevance, instructional design, and effectiveness of this simulation-based activity for learning how to apply decision-making capacity assessment and informed consent principles. Activity The subjects of this study included 140 second-year medical students enrolled in the preclinical curriculum at our institution in 2018–2019. Simulation activities were part of the longitudinal preclinical bioethics course and were designed to reinforce and apply content encountered in first-year bioethics small-group discussion class sessions. Students concurrently participated in a longitudinal clinical skills curriculum and received training in history-taking skills, patient-centered communication, and interpersonal skills. Bioethics and clinical skills course directors collaborated to design the simulation so that learner tasks incorporated previously encountered material and skills from both courses. The overall objective for this activity was to enhance learners’ engagement with bioethics by contextualizing content in realistic clinical encounters that required students to integrate and apply ethical concepts. Prior to the simulation event, a session guide with learning objectives, instructions, and a list of students’ tasks was provided. Presession resources included a self-study review of ethical concepts involved in informed consent, the process of obtaining informed consent to treatment, the elements of decision-making capacity, and how to assess it. Reviews of relevant clinical information previously presented to the students in a different course and information about clinical scenarios were included in presession materials. A video demonstrating a physician obtaining informed consent with a patient was provided for student review. For this 2-h simulation, small groups (4 to 5 students each) rotated through a series of simulated exam rooms for 5 SP encounters. The stem of the clinical scenario was the same for each encounter (a patient presenting to clinic to discuss chemotherapy), but the patient’s attributes (e.g., ability to understand and/or communicate with the provider) and patient’s response to students varied among the 5 SP case scripts; this instructional design was selected to provide learners with contrasting variations in patient presentations in order to help them identify important differences between these cases, thereby highlighting clues to the decision-making capacity of each patient [ 26 – 28 ]. For each encounter, one student role-played the clinician and communicated with the SP, while other students were given various observation tasks. At the completion of the SP encounter, the observing students provided feedback to the lead student on their communication skills performance during the interaction. The student group was also tasked with determining whether or not they assessed the patient as having decision making capacity, for each scenario. At the end of the simulation, students met with the bioethics course instructors for a large-group debrief to reflect on each case scenario, discuss decisional capacity of the patients, and answer questions. Following the debrief, students were emailed a voluntary, online survey evaluating the learning activity. The survey included a series of Likert-scale, multiple-choice questions prompting students to rate the following aspects of the activity: relevance to future role as a physician; whether small-group format, pace, and duration were appropriate; effectiveness of SP encounters for learning about and practicing informed consent and assessment of patient capacity; effectiveness of SP encounters for practicing communications skills; and overall effectiveness of the activity (scale of 1 = poor to 10 = excellent). The survey concluded with free-text, open-ended questions about aspects of the activity that were most effective and what could be improved. Results Forty-one of the 140 students (29%) who participated in the simulation completed the evaluation survey. Learner evaluation survey results indicated that most students (91%) thought that the SP encounter was relevant to their role as a future physician with half of students indicating that the learning event was extremely relevant. When asked to rate overall effectiveness of the activity, 93% of students said the exercise rated ≥ 7 on a scale of 1–10 with 1 being poor and 10 being excellent. All students indicated that the small-group format and activity pace were appropriate. Additional evaluation of the effectiveness of various aspects of the exercise is summarized in Table 1 . Table 1. Learner evaluation results for SP encounter series on informed consent and decisional capacity, October 2018 ( n = 41 respondents, 29%) Question Extremely or quite relevant (%) How relevant was the content of this learning activity to your role asa future physician? 90 Question Yes (%) Was the small-group format of this learning activity appropriate forthe learning content presented? 100 Were the pace and duration of this learning activity appropriate forthe learning content presented? 100 Question : Extremely or quite effective (%) How effective were each of the following aspects of this learning activity? Learning about and practicing informed consent 76 Learning about and practicing how to assess a patient’s capacity 83 Practicing doctor-patient communication skills 93 Open in a new tab Themes among students’ narrative comments included the benefit of practicing bioethics and communication skills in a clinical setting and combining elements of previously presented material from different courses. Students found the preparation materials, feedback from their peers, and discussion in the large-group debrief helpful in reinforcing ethical concepts and providing communication guidance. Students expressed that the experience could be improved by increasing the number of SP encounters for each student, varying the clinical scenarios, and having real-time feedback and debrief from faculty after each encounter in addition to the peer feedback and large-group session. Discussion Learners’ evaluation of this simulation event indicated students perceived the event was highly relevant to their future clinical role as physicians, the event’s instructional design was appropriate, and the SP encounters provided them with an effective opportunity to practice their doctor-patient communication skills while applying the ethical concepts of decision-making capacity assessment and informed consent. Therefore, this activity addressed some primary challenges of learner engagement with ethics in the preclinical curriculum—clinical relevance and practical application. Our study adds to existing literature on bioethics instruction by describing a novel application of simulation for preclerkship medical students to actively engage with and apply concepts of decision-making capacity assessment and informed consent in a low-stakes, formative, clinically relevant setting. Practicing the same skill set during a series of clinical scenarios with contrasting patient presentations (i.e., meaningful variation) enabled students to more easily identify clues to assist them in assessing patient decision-making capacity. This type of exercise can serve as a building block for additional, future instruction on this important skill set. We intentionally designed these cases with a standardized clinical scenario, so students could iteratively practice decisional capacity assessment and communication skills as they reacted to the SP’s responses. However, some students expressed a preference for practicing these skills through a variety of clinical scenarios. Educators should consider using the latter approach, so that learners experience a broader scope of clinical applications for applying these concepts, including some scenarios exploring their nuances. The instructional design for this activity required minimal direct faculty involvement, which is an advantage from a resource utilization standpoint. Although students appreciated the small-group approach with opportunities for peer feedback, some expressed that having an immediate, small-group debriefing with feedback from experienced faculty following each case would have been helpful. Although both designs support a deliberate practice model in which learners receive immediate, specific feedback for ongoing performance refinements, the latter design offers learners an opportunity to glean wisdom, recommendations, and additional examples of real-life application of these ethical concepts from experienced faculty. Therefore, if an educator has access to enough faculty to facilitate, we recommend the latter design. This study was performed at a single institution, and the response rate for the evaluation survey was low, which limit generalizability of our results. Though students’ narrative comments indicated engagement with bioethics material during this learning activity, learner engagement was not formally assessed; additional studies are needed to better characterize learner engagement for this instructional approach versus more traditional methods. Although our data indicated students perceived the activity was effective for practicing the skills, learner reaction data has limited ability to assess effectiveness of an instructional method for impacting learners’ skill level; more objective measures of learners’ performance of these skills (e.g., direct observation assessment of learners during patient encounters) are needed to assess the effectiveness of this instructional approach. This was a single learning event early in students’ training and long-term learning outcomes of this event have not been assessed; additional studies are needed to assess impact on learners’ long-term skill development. Implementation of this learning activity requires access to simulated exam rooms and funding to hire standardized patients, which may limit implementation for educators without direct access to these resources. In conclusion, simulated patient encounters can be an effective means of providing preclinical medical students with opportunities to practice actively applying the concepts of decisional-capacity assessment and informed consent through clinically relevant scenarios, as well as help students appreciate the practical relevance of bioethics to their future clinical roles. Future study is needed to assess the effectiveness of this approach for engaging these learners with other bioethics concepts. Long-term effects of this instructional approach on learner performance of these skills also need to be further studied. Authors’ Contributions All authors contributed to the study conceptualization and design. Material preparation and data collection were performed by Bishop, Jackson, Maradiaga, and Freeman. Data analysis was performed by Bishop and Jackson. The first draft of the manuscript was written by Bishop and Jackson, and all authors commented on previous versions of the manuscript. All authors read and approved the final manuscript. Data Availability Data collected is non-identifiable and secured with institutional password protection in survey program Compliance with Ethical Standards Conflict of Interest The authors declare that they have no conflicts of interest. Ethics Approval All procedures performed in studies involving human participants were in accordance with the ethical standards of the institutional research committee (Wake Forest School of Medicine Institutional Review Board, IRB00050949) and with the 1964 Helsinki declaration and its later amendments or comparable ethical standards. Consent to Participate Informed consent given with completion of survey Consent for Publication Not applicable Code Availability Not applicable Footnotes Publisher’s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. LCME. Functions and Structure of a Medical School: Standards for accreditation of Medical Education Programs Leading to the MD Degree. http://lcme.org/wp-content/uploads/filebase/standards/2019-20_Functions-and-Structure_2018-09-26.docx . 2. Lehmann LS, Kasoff WS, Koch P, Federman DD. A survey of medical ethics education at U.S. and Canadian medical schools. Acad Med. 2004;79(7):682–689. doi: 10.1097/00001888-200407000-00015. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Giubilini A, Milnes S, Savulescu J. The medical ethics curriculum in medical schools: present and future. J Clin Ethics. 2016;27(2):129–145. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Carrese JA, Malek J, Watson K, Lehmann LS, Green MJ, McCullough LB, et al. The essential role of medical ethics education in achieving professionalism: the Romanell Report. Acad Med. 2015;90(6):744–752. doi: 10.1097/acm.0000000000000715. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Eckles RE, Meslin EM, Gaffney M, Helft PR. Medical ethics education: where are we? Where should we be going? A review. Acad Med. 2005;80(12):1143–1152. doi: 10.1097/00001888-200512000-00020. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Goldie J. Review of ethics curricula in undergraduate medical education. Med Educ. 2000;34(2):108–119. doi: 10